WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.153 --> 00:00:01.392
- Good morning, Believe Nation!

00:00:01.392 --> 00:00:05.172
Today's message is strive to improve.

00:00:05.172 --> 00:00:07.186
Over to you, Karl Lagerfeld.

00:00:07.186 --> 00:00:09.449
(rooster crows)

00:00:09.449 --> 00:00:12.091
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:12.091 --> 00:00:14.899
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:00:14.899 --> 00:00:18.830
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:21.893
- I'm never pleased with myself.

00:00:21.893 --> 00:00:24.101
I always think I could do
better, I could make an effort.

00:00:24.101 --> 00:00:24.934
I'm lazy.

00:00:24.934 --> 00:00:26.312
I mean, it's ridiculous, I know.

00:00:26.312 --> 00:00:27.888
But I have the healthy attitude

00:00:27.888 --> 00:00:32.123
and I don't think I should
change my state of mind.

00:00:32.123 --> 00:00:35.263
Because when people start
to think about their past,

00:00:35.263 --> 00:00:37.011
their art, and all this,
it's very dangerous.

00:00:37.011 --> 00:00:40.065
I do a kind of applied
art, don't be pretentious.

00:00:40.065 --> 00:00:43.465
And the good thing is that I could never,

00:00:43.465 --> 00:00:46.253
like the old Rolling Stones song,

00:00:46.253 --> 00:00:48.125
any satisfaction of what I'm doing.

00:00:48.125 --> 00:00:50.744
Always think it could
improve, I could do better,

00:00:50.744 --> 00:00:51.782
and I could do more.

00:00:51.782 --> 00:00:54.446
That's ridiculous, but
it's very healthy, I think.

00:00:54.446 --> 00:00:56.365
- I think that is a
trait that you will see

00:00:56.365 --> 00:00:59.852
across all successful people,
that it's never good enough,

00:00:59.852 --> 00:01:02.056
that they want to improve,
that they want to get better,

00:01:02.056 --> 00:01:04.135
that they're always looking
for feedback and criticism

00:01:04.135 --> 00:01:05.285
on what they could do better,

00:01:05.285 --> 00:01:07.604
and they are their worst critic.

00:01:07.604 --> 00:01:10.567
No matter what you say, how
you try to tear somebody down,

00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:12.446
they are harder on themselves.

00:01:12.446 --> 00:01:14.615
Successful people are harder on themselves

00:01:14.615 --> 00:01:16.491
than anything that you
could throw at them,

00:01:16.491 --> 00:01:18.225
and that's what leads to their success.

00:01:18.225 --> 00:01:20.855
I've been thinking a lot
about celebration recently,

00:01:20.855 --> 00:01:23.118
because I don't celebrate a lot

00:01:23.118 --> 00:01:24.760
and probably don't celebrate enough.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.583
And as I research it more,

00:01:26.583 --> 00:01:28.868
you see that the most successful people

00:01:28.868 --> 00:01:31.784
don't actually celebrate a lot.

00:01:31.784 --> 00:01:32.914
They don't celebrate a ton,

00:01:32.914 --> 00:01:36.743
because what they're
doing is not good enough

00:01:36.743 --> 00:01:38.627
in their mind yet, right?

00:01:38.627 --> 00:01:40.111
I'm wearing a Blue Jay cap.

00:01:40.111 --> 00:01:42.397
And here we go, go, Jays, go.

00:01:42.397 --> 00:01:44.086
- Go, Jays, go.

00:01:44.086 --> 00:01:45.027
- I love baseball.

00:01:45.027 --> 00:01:47.914
For some people, just
getting to the Major Leagues,

00:01:47.914 --> 00:01:49.315
that's a win.

00:01:49.315 --> 00:01:50.148
That's a win.

00:01:50.148 --> 00:01:51.337
Like, "I made it.

00:01:51.337 --> 00:01:53.333
"I made it to the Major Leagues.

00:01:53.333 --> 00:01:54.990
"I hit my goal."

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:59.266
And for some people, playing
a full season is their goal,

00:01:59.266 --> 00:02:00.099
and they hit a win.

00:02:00.099 --> 00:02:02.166
And for some people, just
make it into the playoffs.

00:02:02.166 --> 00:02:04.046
They can say, "I made it to the playoffs."

00:02:04.046 --> 00:02:06.344
And for some people, just
playing in the World Series.

00:02:06.344 --> 00:02:08.139
"I played in the World Series."

00:02:08.139 --> 00:02:10.112
That's a win for them.

00:02:10.112 --> 00:02:12.162
But there are other people

00:02:12.162 --> 00:02:15.091
who want to be one of
the greatest of all time.

00:02:15.091 --> 00:02:17.265
They want to be one of
the greats of the great.

00:02:17.265 --> 00:02:20.207
In their head, even if it's delusional,

00:02:20.207 --> 00:02:22.896
in their head they see
themselves winning five, six,

00:02:22.896 --> 00:02:25.066
seven, 10 World Series.

00:02:25.066 --> 00:02:26.120
That's where they want to get.

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:28.393
Unless they get that,
they're not satisfied

00:02:28.393 --> 00:02:30.168
so they're striving to improve.

00:02:30.168 --> 00:02:32.074
Improve themselves,
improve their teammates,

00:02:32.074 --> 00:02:34.825
improve their strategy,
improve to get to that point.

00:02:34.825 --> 00:02:37.934
Because in their mind
they're winning five, six,

00:02:37.934 --> 00:02:41.605
seven, eight, nine, 10 World Series

00:02:41.605 --> 00:02:44.105
And so if they win one, great.

00:02:45.658 --> 00:02:47.245
It's a win, hey, good.

00:02:47.245 --> 00:02:49.333
Celebrate one day, awesome, good.

00:02:49.333 --> 00:02:51.544
But it's only a step along the way.

00:02:51.544 --> 00:02:52.914
'Cause in their mind they want to be one

00:02:52.914 --> 00:02:55.056
of the greatest of all time.

00:02:55.056 --> 00:02:58.958
And so one World Series is not enough.

00:02:58.958 --> 00:03:00.866
They want to get better and improve.

00:03:00.866 --> 00:03:02.992
And it doesn't have to be
from an ego point of view,

00:03:02.992 --> 00:03:05.341
like I want to be the best.

00:03:05.341 --> 00:03:07.409
It could also be from
an impact point of view.

00:03:07.409 --> 00:03:09.949
One of the things I
remind myself every day

00:03:09.949 --> 00:03:12.473
is I have the ability to change the world

00:03:12.473 --> 00:03:15.787
and it's my responsibility to do so.

00:03:15.787 --> 00:03:19.454
It's my responsibility to
get better at what I do

00:03:19.454 --> 00:03:22.541
to serve more people, to
help more entrepreneurs.

00:03:22.541 --> 00:03:24.103
I need to get better at my videos.

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:26.070
Great, we crossed half
a million subscribers.

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:29.270
Amazing, quick celebration, but let's go.

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:30.627
That's not enough.

00:03:30.627 --> 00:03:32.072
A million, five million, 10 million.

00:03:32.072 --> 00:03:33.705
It's not about hitting those numbers.

00:03:33.705 --> 00:03:35.380
It's about the impact that it can have.

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:37.673
It's about reaching
people with a positive,

00:03:37.673 --> 00:03:39.639
inspiring, empowering message

00:03:39.639 --> 00:03:41.406
for them to go and do something amazing

00:03:41.406 --> 00:03:45.421
instead of wasting their time
watching gag videos, right?

00:03:45.421 --> 00:03:48.004
Instead of escaping your world,

00:03:49.901 --> 00:03:53.041
instead of escaping your job

00:03:53.041 --> 00:03:55.842
and having to turn to
watching funny videos

00:03:55.842 --> 00:03:59.885
or trying to go on vacation as an escape,

00:03:59.885 --> 00:04:02.692
I want you to not want
to escape your life.

00:04:02.692 --> 00:04:04.525
I want you to live a life with purpose

00:04:04.525 --> 00:04:06.342
and passion and meaning.

00:04:06.342 --> 00:04:07.630
And that's on me to get better

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:08.898
to reach more people to do it.

00:04:08.898 --> 00:04:10.429
A lot of you guys are already onboard

00:04:10.429 --> 00:04:12.012
and you're already taking steps

00:04:12.012 --> 00:04:14.015
and you resonate strongly
with the message.

00:04:14.015 --> 00:04:15.556
There's a lot of people who don't get it

00:04:15.556 --> 00:04:16.682
and I'm not good enough yet

00:04:16.682 --> 00:04:18.809
to be able to reach all of those people

00:04:18.809 --> 00:04:19.747
and I need to get better.

00:04:19.747 --> 00:04:21.227
That's how I see it.

00:04:21.227 --> 00:04:22.120
I need to get better.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:23.638
Every day I need to improve.

00:04:23.638 --> 00:04:25.654
I want to have that kind of impact

00:04:25.654 --> 00:04:28.279
where I'm winning the five, six, seven,

00:04:28.279 --> 00:04:29.697
eight, nine, 10 World Series,

00:04:29.697 --> 00:04:31.283
so hitting one isn't enough.

00:04:31.283 --> 00:04:33.934
And that's why the big time winners,

00:04:33.934 --> 00:04:37.146
the guys and girls who are
having that big impact,

00:04:37.146 --> 00:04:41.313
they can't relate to the
person who their biggest moment

00:04:42.249 --> 00:04:45.614
in their life was getting to the game,

00:04:45.614 --> 00:04:47.908
to playing their first
game in the Major Leagues.

00:04:47.908 --> 00:04:50.109
If that's your ceiling,

00:04:50.109 --> 00:04:52.509
that's the ceiling that
you imposed on yourself.

00:04:52.509 --> 00:04:56.581
That's the biggest win in
your life, getting there.

00:04:56.581 --> 00:04:57.790
You just can't relate.

00:04:57.790 --> 00:05:00.061
I don't want to celebrate that.

00:05:00.061 --> 00:05:02.831
I want to celebrate
doing much bigger things.

00:05:02.831 --> 00:05:07.080
And so I think having that
mindset of striving to improve,

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:08.457
of it never being good enough,

00:05:08.457 --> 00:05:10.487
about there always being
a way to make it better,

00:05:10.487 --> 00:05:13.179
about taking the personal
responsibility, right?

00:05:13.179 --> 00:05:16.184
If I look at my videos, there's
a team of people around me

00:05:16.184 --> 00:05:17.559
who are helping me make these,

00:05:17.559 --> 00:05:20.183
and it's on all of them to get better too,

00:05:20.183 --> 00:05:22.424
but it's not their fault
for not getting better,

00:05:22.424 --> 00:05:23.511
it's my fault.

00:05:23.511 --> 00:05:26.119
It's my fault for not coaching
them, for training them,

00:05:26.119 --> 00:05:28.851
for improving them, for
getting them the mindset

00:05:28.851 --> 00:05:30.148
and motivation to get better.

00:05:30.148 --> 00:05:30.981
And it's on me.

00:05:30.981 --> 00:05:32.780
I'm doing the product.

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:33.841
It's on me to get better.

00:05:33.841 --> 00:05:36.715
This conversation has to get better,

00:05:36.715 --> 00:05:38.406
has to be more impactful.

00:05:38.406 --> 00:05:41.353
And so I think if you look
at any successful person,

00:05:41.353 --> 00:05:44.616
people who have reached the
heights that they're at,

00:05:44.616 --> 00:05:46.391
they don't just sit back.

00:05:46.391 --> 00:05:47.975
They'll recognize it for a little bit,

00:05:47.975 --> 00:05:49.690
but then let's move forward.

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:50.597
Let's make this better.

00:05:50.597 --> 00:05:51.526
How do we improve?

00:05:51.526 --> 00:05:54.858
How do we have a bigger
impact on this planet?

00:05:54.858 --> 00:05:57.702
And I sincerely hope that for you as well.

00:05:57.702 --> 00:05:59.643
So the question of the
day today is I'm curious,

00:05:59.643 --> 00:06:02.893
how much time do you spend celebrating?

00:06:04.570 --> 00:06:05.522
Leave it in the comments below.

00:06:05.522 --> 00:06:06.892
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:06:06.892 --> 00:06:08.137
I also want to give a quick shout-out

00:06:08.137 --> 00:06:10.084
to William from nucor.com.

00:06:10.084 --> 00:06:11.246
William, thank you so much

00:06:11.246 --> 00:06:13.775
for picking up a copy of
my book, Your One Word.

00:06:13.775 --> 00:06:15.617
It really, really, really
means a lot to me, man.

00:06:15.617 --> 00:06:17.176
I appreciate the support.

00:06:17.176 --> 00:06:18.586
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:06:18.586 --> 00:06:19.697
I believe in you.

00:06:19.697 --> 00:06:21.745
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:06:21.745 --> 00:06:23.855
and whatever your one word is.

00:06:23.855 --> 00:06:26.080
Much love, and I'll see
you again tomorrow morning

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:28.667
for another shot of Entspresso.

00:06:28.667 --> 00:06:31.359
♫ I wake up every morning

00:06:31.359 --> 00:06:34.892
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:06:34.892 --> 00:06:37.965
- Hey, Believe Nation, if
you want some more videos

00:06:37.965 --> 00:06:39.442
on striving to improve

00:06:39.442 --> 00:06:41.850
to really lock in that message in here,

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:45.888
I think you're going to
enjoy these bonus clips.

00:06:45.888 --> 00:06:50.311
- Boy, this is the first
time in years. (laughs)

00:06:50.311 --> 00:06:51.356
Doing this all from memory.

00:06:51.356 --> 00:06:54.285
This is hard to remember
an old computer language.

00:06:54.285 --> 00:06:57.368
(gentle piano music)

00:06:58.603 --> 00:07:00.336
I not only designed this,

00:07:00.336 --> 00:07:02.707
every little metal trace
that you see on here,

00:07:02.707 --> 00:07:05.387
I strapped onto a drafting table

00:07:05.387 --> 00:07:08.141
and I built this whole board myself

00:07:08.141 --> 00:07:12.308
because I wanted the chips in
the ultimate perfect position.

00:07:14.045 --> 00:07:15.554
I worked for a couple of weeks every night

00:07:15.554 --> 00:07:17.209
till about four in the morning,

00:07:17.209 --> 00:07:19.301
laying out the little things
on a big drafting table

00:07:19.301 --> 00:07:21.401
that would eventually become this board.

00:07:21.401 --> 00:07:22.234
And when I got done,

00:07:22.234 --> 00:07:25.466
I realized I had to drill
eight holes in the board,

00:07:25.466 --> 00:07:26.697
and I said, "You know what?

00:07:26.697 --> 00:07:29.509
"If I had only designed it
a little bit differently,

00:07:29.509 --> 00:07:33.249
"then I would've only had five holes."

00:07:33.249 --> 00:07:35.106
I tore apart my entire design,

00:07:35.106 --> 00:07:36.162
and for another two weeks,

00:07:36.162 --> 00:07:37.665
every night till four in the morning

00:07:37.665 --> 00:07:40.034
to get it just that perfect
with only five holes

00:07:40.034 --> 00:07:41.356
so you wouldn't ever
see the three missing.

00:07:41.356 --> 00:07:43.587
It had to be that
artistically perfect to me,

00:07:43.587 --> 00:07:47.091
because it represents yourself
when you do a great design.

00:07:47.091 --> 00:07:49.627
- Whatever you really,
really, really want, you get.

00:07:49.627 --> 00:07:54.242
And whatever you really, really,
really don't want, you get.

00:07:54.242 --> 00:07:56.342
Because whatever your mind is focusing on,

00:07:56.342 --> 00:07:58.978
you pull these things into your life.

00:07:58.978 --> 00:08:01.948
So your perspective is so important.

00:08:01.948 --> 00:08:04.431
And for me it was a slow,
slow, slow, slow build,

00:08:04.431 --> 00:08:05.987
but I got me out of that depression

00:08:05.987 --> 00:08:07.987
and really lifted me up.

00:08:10.875 --> 00:08:13.902
And I don't lose, you
go like this, you know?

00:08:13.902 --> 00:08:16.405
It's not just a straight, you know?

00:08:16.405 --> 00:08:20.572
But I've always kept that
desire, you know what I mean?

00:08:21.974 --> 00:08:24.339
And it's a sincere desire.

00:08:24.339 --> 00:08:26.071
And when you ask for something like that,

00:08:26.071 --> 00:08:28.846
you start seeing things about yourself.

00:08:28.846 --> 00:08:31.121
Because really the way
the world is relative.

00:08:31.121 --> 00:08:35.288
It's the way you see yourself
that shapes the world.

00:08:37.249 --> 00:08:38.089
It doesn't shape the world,

00:08:38.089 --> 00:08:39.656
it shapes your perspective on the world.

00:08:39.656 --> 00:08:42.545
So if you want to continually change,

00:08:42.545 --> 00:08:44.962
you have to have that desire.

00:08:47.482 --> 00:08:50.745
I can pinpoint certain points in my life

00:08:50.745 --> 00:08:54.439
where you have these moments of clarity.

00:08:54.439 --> 00:08:58.294
I was talking about the academic portion,

00:08:58.294 --> 00:09:01.123
the clarity of the guitar.

00:09:01.123 --> 00:09:04.640
We all have these very
subtle, intuitive moments

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:07.002
of clarity, but you have to want them.

00:09:07.002 --> 00:09:09.031
If you're stuck in a
particular way of thinking

00:09:09.031 --> 00:09:11.256
for your whole life, you're
not going to get them.

00:09:11.256 --> 00:09:13.395
- [Interviewer] You have to see them.

00:09:13.395 --> 00:09:16.022
- You have to want to be.

00:09:16.022 --> 00:09:18.841
You have to want to improve
yourself as a person.

00:09:18.841 --> 00:09:20.435
- Well, it's not about not killing.

00:09:20.435 --> 00:09:22.798
It's about doing the
best show that you can do

00:09:22.798 --> 00:09:26.965
and it's about staying at
the edge of your ability.

00:09:27.883 --> 00:09:30.057
I want to do a better show
every time I'm onstage.

00:09:30.057 --> 00:09:32.982
I mean, part of it is that I want

00:09:32.982 --> 00:09:35.236
what I do to keep getting better.

00:09:35.236 --> 00:09:36.899
Unless you're think
you're the best comedian

00:09:36.899 --> 00:09:38.043
that ever could possibly,

00:09:38.043 --> 00:09:40.859
like your perfect, crystalline
diamond of a comedian

00:09:40.859 --> 00:09:43.111
that couldn't possibly be improved upon.

00:09:43.111 --> 00:09:45.391
Every molecule has been
compressed together

00:09:45.391 --> 00:09:47.359
to the point there's no air in it.

00:09:47.359 --> 00:09:50.477
You're a black hole of a comic.

00:09:50.477 --> 00:09:53.048
There's just no room for improvement.

00:09:53.048 --> 00:09:55.864
You folded the samurai sword 1,000 times,

00:09:55.864 --> 00:09:57.843
then just can't fold anymore.

00:09:57.843 --> 00:10:00.196
No, so you got to keep trying.

00:10:00.196 --> 00:10:02.242
I mean, I think a lot of
people in show business,

00:10:02.242 --> 00:10:05.854
and comedy even especially, are very lazy

00:10:05.854 --> 00:10:08.856
and they just sort of think
that you just get up there

00:10:08.856 --> 00:10:11.221
and then you wait for your
manager to get you a better one,

00:10:11.221 --> 00:10:12.454
a better shot.

00:10:12.454 --> 00:10:15.805
But it's something you got to really,

00:10:15.805 --> 00:10:20.232
to me, I mean, for me I just
want to keep working at it.

00:10:20.232 --> 00:10:23.665
Yeah, I wouldn't like it if
I stopped trying really hard.

00:10:23.665 --> 00:10:26.827
It isn't a neurosis like, "Oh,
geez, I hope they like me."

00:10:26.827 --> 00:10:28.536
It's this is what I do,

00:10:28.536 --> 00:10:30.681
I got to do it really well.

00:10:30.681 --> 00:10:34.902
- For me, I would say it's
all driven by curiosity

00:10:34.902 --> 00:10:37.737
and the desire to be
personally challenged.

00:10:37.737 --> 00:10:38.570
Those two things.

00:10:38.570 --> 00:10:39.822
I can sum it all up.

00:10:39.822 --> 00:10:43.746
And i don't know if it's
a formula for success,

00:10:43.746 --> 00:10:45.937
it's just what drives me.

00:10:45.937 --> 00:10:48.441
I'm very curious, I want to know more.

00:10:48.441 --> 00:10:51.073
I chose projects that
I'm going to learn from.

00:10:51.073 --> 00:10:52.780
I choose projects that are challenging.

00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:55.027
I don't want to do the same thing twice.

00:10:55.027 --> 00:10:57.787
So I think personal challenge,

00:10:57.787 --> 00:11:00.923
trying to strive for excellence.

00:11:00.923 --> 00:11:04.703
And to me, in filmmaking the excellence is

00:11:04.703 --> 00:11:08.786
how excellent can the
entertainment experience be

00:11:10.425 --> 00:11:12.231
for the audience?

00:11:12.231 --> 00:11:14.231
I'll pull out all stops.

